title: Hoi An's lantern boat ride — why some pay $6 and others $20 (2026)
format: story
charstyle: nutshell
voice: af_heart
music: night
score: night@1 wonder@2 upbeat@3 comedy@4 chill@5 wonder@6 emotional@7 night@8 lofi@9 tension@11 upbeat@12
sources: Vietadvisor "Is the Hoi An Lantern Boat Ride Really Worth It?" Sep 2026 (170,000 VND per boat for 1–3, 220,000 for 4–5; ~20 min, sometimes 15; ~4pm–9:30pm, last boats ~9pm; booths on Bach Dang and Nguyen Phuc Chu near An Hoi Bridge; you can get off on the other side if you tell the rower; lanterns 5–15k extra, rower hands you a pole; online listings ~$7.85–10.42 per person; full moon on the 14th lunar day, street lights off; 6–7pm busiest, 8:30 shorter queue; Oct–Nov floods stop boats; night mode, shoot while stopped; Ancient Town ticket 120k separate); trvlogue.com Hoi An lantern boat guide, Sep 2026 (170k / 220k per boat, ~20 min, 4–9:30pm, lanterns ~10k); danangfantasticity.com Ancient Town tickets 2026 (80k and 120k tickets, valid up to 3 days). Checked Oct 2026.
// YOUTUBE CUT (16:9), v11: no mascot, no standing cast; each shot bespoke with its own camera. One continuous evening, no chapters.
// VIRAL   "$6 or $20?" at 1.3 s; per-boat twist (0:40); the 3× scale; the on-here-off-there trick (re-hook); full moon; save card.

// 0:00 MACRO match lights a silk lantern → WHIP OUT aerial river; "$6 OR $20?"; "SAME BOAT. SAME RIVER."
# vis kind=ha act=hook
Twenty minutes on a lantern boat in Hoi An.
Some people pay six dollars for it. Some pay twenty.
Same boat. Same river. Same twenty minutes.
// 0:12 RIPPLE into the aerial map: two bridges, a boat draws the 20-minute route.
# vis kind=ha act=river trans=ripple tc=.5,.5
The boats are hand-rowed wooden sampans on the Hoai River, looping between the An Hoi and Cam Nam bridges.
A ride is about twenty minutes, sometimes closer to fifteen, from around four in the afternoon until half past nine.
// 0:28 BLOBS to the isometric kiosk: price board fills, coins, ticket.
# vis kind=ha act=booth trans=blobs
The tickets are sold at small booths along Bach Dang and Nguyen Phuc Chu streets, near the An Hoi bridge, and the price is per boat.
One to three people: one hundred and seventy thousand dong.
Four or five: two hundred and twenty thousand. There's nothing to haggle over.
// 0:48 WHIP to the POV phone → the brass scale tips → "3× MORE".
# vis kind=ha act=phone trans=whip
Online, booking sites usually sell the very same ride per person, at roughly eight to ten dollars a head.
So two people pay sixteen to twenty-one dollars.
Walk to a booth, and the same two people pay about six and a half.
// 1:06 STRIPES back to the aerial: a pin at one bridge, a pin at the other — board here, get off there.
# vis kind=ha act=route trans=stripes2
Here's the trick almost nobody mentions: you don't have to come back to where you started.
Tell the rower before you leave, board near the An Hoi bridge, and get off on the night market side.
// 1:18 LIQUID into the water-level macro release.
# vis kind=ha act=release trans=liquid tcol=#1a1a5a
The floating lanterns aren't part of the fare, whatever some listings suggest.
Buy them from the sellers on the bank for five to fifteen thousand each. The rower will hand you a pole to set yours down.
// 1:32 BLOOM up into the moonrise: lights click off, the river fills.
# vis kind=ha act=moon trans=bloom tc=.62,.3 tcol=#fff2c8
Once a month, on the fourteenth day of the lunar calendar, the town switches off its street lights for the full moon.
The river fills with lanterns. It's the best night for the ride, and the night with the longest queues.
// 1:47 SLICES to the riverfront clock: six to eight-thirty, the queue shrinks.
# vis kind=ha act=clock trans=slices
On any night, six to seven is the prettiest hour, with colour still in the sky, and the busiest.
Come at half past eight, and the wait is far shorter on the same river.
// 1:58 WHIP into the POV camera: blurry while moving, sharp when the boat stops for the lanterns.
# vis kind=ha act=photo trans=whip
Phones struggle with this light. Use night mode and hold still.
Take your pictures while the boat has stopped for the lantern release, not while it's moving.
// 2:10 DROP onto the macro ticket punch: "SEPARATE".
# vis kind=ha act=ticket trans=drop
One more cost: the Ancient Town ticket isn't included either.
That's one hundred and twenty thousand dong, and it's valid for up to three days.
// 2:20 SPIN into the rainy flood cutaway.
# vis kind=ha act=flood trans=spin
And in October and November, when the river rises, the boats simply stop, usually with no warning at all.
If you book online in those months, pick free cancellation.
// 2:32 ZOOM THROUGH into the turning lantern save card.
# vis kind=ha act=rules trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.5 items="BUY AT THE BOOTH|PER BOAT, NOT PER PERSON|GO AT 8:30"
So: buy at the booth.
Pay per boat, not per person.
And go at half past eight.
// 2:42 MATCH into the turquoise bay: "NEXT: PHU QUOC".
# vis kind=ha act=tease trans=match tc=.5,.5
Next time: Phu Quoc.
